sketch-orca oracle patch 0003: the thread knob, SKETCH_ORCA_ORACLE_THREADS.
Modified for Sketch, 2026-11-06.

With SKETCH_ORCA_ORACLE_THREADS=P, a whole number from 1 to 256, the front end's main runs the
whole run inside a tbb::task_arena of concurrency P, while a
tbb::global_control(max_allowed_parallelism, 1), held until the run ends, leaves TBB no worker
thread. TBB then splits the work as for P threads (this_task_arena::max_concurrency() reports P to
TreeSupport3D.cpp:2385, TreeModelVolumes.cpp and the partitioners) and does all of it on the main
thread, in one order, the same on every run. All P of the arena's slots are reserved for
application threads, so it asks TBB for no worker: asking for P - 1 under that limit would make
TBB print a warning to stderr on a machine without P hardware threads. utils.cpp:196-204 is left
as it is: its disable_multi_threading builds a temporary global_control, which does nothing.
name_tbb_thread_pool_threads_set_locale (Thread.cpp), which Print::process calls, returns at once
when the knob is set: there are no worker threads to name, and its barrier, which waits until
max_concurrency() tasks run at the same time, would wait forever on one thread. Whether the arena
reports P is recorded at every dump (patch 0005, the RUN record) and by parity neutral. A value
that is not such a number stops the oracle before it does anything else, with exit code 78.
Unset, nothing changes.

---
